<title>AUSTRALIA: RAIN HALTS DETERIORATION OF AUSTRALIAN WHEAT CROP.</title>
<headline>RAIN HALTS DETERIORATION OF AUSTRALIAN WHEAT CROP.</headline>
<dateline>SYDNEY 1997-08-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Reasonable overnight rainfall across most of Australia's drought-stricken wheatbelt has rescued the crop from further deterioration but more rain is needed, grains analysts told Reuters.  </p>
<p>Rain fell across most of Australia's eastern and southern wheatbelt, with falls more widespread but lighter than hoped.</p>
<p>A spokesman for the Bureau of Meteorology in New South Wales said rain fell across most of the state, with most falls in the 10-15 millimetres range.</p>
<p>Rainfall in South Australia and Victoria was also mostly in the same range, although some falls were up to about 40 mm in the western agriculture area of South Australia.</p>
</text>
